/*! \page developer_backend_page Supporting Multiple Backends

\ref developer_containers_page (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_param_page      (Next)
<BR>

The PSCF package builds two executable files, that are pscf_rpc and 
pscf_rpg, that are designed to solve the same set of problems involving 
real periodic fields, but that are specialized to use different hardware. 
The pscf_rpg executable performs makes use of a GPU, while the pscf_rpg 
executable uses only a CPU. The primary difference in the implementation 
of these two programs arises differences in code that implements a few 
basic mathematical operations that are performed on large arrays, such 
as elementwise linear algebra operations on arrays, array reductions, and 
fast Fourier transforms.  In pscf_rpg, these operations are performed on 
a GPU, where in pscf_rpc they are performed on the CPU.  

We describe these differences in what follows by saying that analogous 
executable files use different computational "backends" for these core
mathematical operations. The subpages linked below describe the design 
of the conventions used in the PSCF source to allow the use of different 
backends with minimal code duplication. As described in more detail in 
these subpages, the system used by PSCF relies heavily on the use of 
C++ templates.

<ol>
 <li> \subpage developer_backend_id_page </li>
 <li> \subpage developer_backend_field_page </li>
 <li> \subpage developer_backend_rp_page </li>
</ol>


